WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.860
Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle turned out
far better than anyone had any right to expect.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:11.870
What initially sounded soulless and cynical
ended up being a massively successful, well-received,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.910
family-friendly thrill ride for kids and adults
alike.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:19.689
Starring Dwayne Johnson, Kevin Hart, Jack
Black, and Karen Gillan, the movie effectively

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:26.050
built on the 1995 story about a magical board
game with a video-game-based, body-swap comedy

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.050
sequel.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.019
Welcome to the Jungle made a lot out of a
wacky premise, but that doesn't mean it was

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.019
perfect.

00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:36.570
Many of its quirks won't be picked up by younger
viewers, but the adults in the room probably

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.739
noticed them.

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:42.980
Here are some of the things only grown-ups
will notice in Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.809
What console is that?

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:50.899
Jumanji opens in 1996, introducing viewers
to a teenage boy named Alex in a sleeveless

00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:55.680
Metallica tee whose bedroom is covered in
pop culture posters from the era.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.199
Nothing says "1996 teenager" like his quick
dismissal of the Jumanji board game in favor

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:03.800
of his trusty PlayStation controller.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.299
We see he's playing Twisted Metal, a game
that was released for the Playstation in November

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:09.750
1995.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.049
But when we pause to marvel at the all the
authentic 90s stuff in his room, we get a

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:16.600
good look at the console itself.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.380
Spoiler alert: that's no PlayStation.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.060
It doesn't even remotely resemble a PlayStation.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.340
From the looks of things, its closest twin
is the Atari 2600, a wood-paneled console

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.420
that was already 20 years old in the mid-90s.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.899
And of course, since you've noticed it once,
you're not going to be able to ignore that

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:38.479
the controllers go from PlayStation to vaguely
Nintendo-esque when the kids pull the game

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:39.860
out at the school.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.159
Fittingly enough, they can't make heads or
tails of this odd contraption either.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:47.200
"What is it, some kind of old-school Nintendo
or something?"

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.130
"I don't think so.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.540
I don't recognize it."

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.180
Have your cheesecake

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:59.840
It seems to be a common belief that just acknowledging
overdone clichéd tropes is enough to subvert

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.840
them in movies or shows, but that's not really
always the case.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.270
Jumanji uses Martha as a mouthpiece to criticize
the unnecessarily skimpy outfit worn by her

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.940
avatar, Ruby Roundhouse.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.520
But, as good a sentiment as this might be,
the film is making this commentary on how

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.170
women are designed in video games while it
simultaneously benefits from having a young

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.030
actress in a skimpy outfit.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.850
Fortunately, the movie is brimming with charm,
and doesn't rely too heavily on sexy shots

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.130
of a woman's body to keep people watching.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:33.290
In fact, she adds bits of her male companions'
outfits to her own in order to feel a bit

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:37.640
more comfortable as she journeys through the
jungle kicking bad guy butt with her dance

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.050
fighting techniques.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.690
But the marketing for the movie has no such
subtlety, making this a noticeable instance

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.030
of Jumanji wanting to have its cake and eat
it too.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.470
"Parrish" the thought

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.200
At the midpoint in the film, when the teens
have been set upon by corrupt researcher Russel

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.570
Van Pelt and there's no obvious way to move
forward, the missing teenager Alex shows up

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.770
in the avatar of Jefferson "Seaplane" McDonough.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:08.010
He leads our heroes through a hallway full
of authentically video gamey traps to his

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.820
little hideaway set apart from the main Jumanji
playing area.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.570
He's been holed up there for who knows how
long, keeping his head down and trying to

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.210
hold onto his last life.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.400
He explains to the new kids that the little
bungalow where he lives, drinking delicious

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.530
margaritas, is not a structure of his own
making.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.310
Instead, it once belonged to a man named Alan
Parrish.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:33.130
Alan Parrish is, of course, Robin Williams'
character from the original Jumanji.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:37.600
Not only is the reference a lovely tip of
the hat to Alan's character, it also gives

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.410
fans of the original movie a glimpse at how
Alan was living during his years away.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.580
Alex's amazing slang

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:45.970
"She's pretty fly."

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.130
"She da bomb."

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.130
"I'm just saying.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.930
I'm sure they'd like to get jiggy with her."

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:55.260
Alex's tortured 90s slang is out of place
in the jungle, and also this century.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.980
But his lame-o vocabulary helps the four kids
realize that the Alex they've found inside

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:06.630
the game is Alex Vreeke, the boy who's been
missing, and stuck in Jumanji, for 20 years.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:11.090
It's not a bad idea to use outdated slang
to tip off the characters that Alex is from

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.300
another era, but there are a few problems
here.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.370
One is that Will Smith's "Gettin' Jiggy Wit
It" came out in 1997, not 1996.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:25.170
Plus, exactly zero people have ever actually
used the phrase "gettin' jiggy" in conversation.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.310
Ever.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.310
Ask your parents.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.010
Also, if they were going to go there with
that style of slang, they probably should

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.760
have gone with a different characterization
for Alex.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.039
Instead of a Metallica tee and a grungy hairstyle,
he probably should have been wearing a Cross

00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:40.720
Colours tank top and some radical prints.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.689
The walls of his bedroom should have been
covered in TLC and Michael Jordan posters.

00:04:44.689 --> 00:04:46.539
Now that's 90s.

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:49.270
Instead, his lingo comes off as forced.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.930
Everyone knows mid-90s metalheads didn't say
anything was "da bomb!"

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.020
Then again… no one really knows what they
say sometimes.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:01.780
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.000
The empty Fridge

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.270
While Kevin Hart does good work as Fridge's
avatar, Fridge himself has no real arc, and

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:13.060
learns no valuable life lessons in the movie.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.229
Things start off promising.

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:18.759
We learn that Fridge asked Spencer to do his
homework as a favor, which turns out to be

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:20.320
a bad idea.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.830
Spencer may be a nerd, but he's not necessarily
a good student, and the bogus paper he provides

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.960
for Fridge is easily sniffed out by his teacher.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.819
As a result, Fridge gets kicked off of the
football team.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:35.340
Later, when Fridge and Spencer's tension comes
to a head on a cliff in Jumanji, they get

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.810
into a shoving match, costing Spencer one
of his lives.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.499
But by working together through the rest of
the adventure, they gain a mutual respect

00:05:42.499 --> 00:05:43.509
for one another.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:48.419
Still, at the end of the movie, the problems
that led to that cliff confrontation are still

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:49.419
there.

00:05:49.419 --> 00:05:51.690
The boys haven't hashed out their personal
conflict.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:55.960
Fridge doesn't apologize for using Spencer
to do his homework, nor for insulting and

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.530
intimidating him in Jumanji.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:01.569
They don't team up in the real world to get
Fridge reinstated to the football team.

00:06:01.569 --> 00:06:05.620
There's no shot of Fridge working hard on
his own homework, or doing anything to wrap

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.979
up the problems that got him in detention
in the first place.

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:12.810
All that's changed about him at the end of
his bogus journey with Spencer, Bethany, and

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:16.039
Martha is that he seems nicer and vaguely
content.

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:20.060
The movie doesn't want you to notice it, but
his academic and athletic future is still

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.939
severely compromised by the movie's end.

00:06:22.939 --> 00:06:26.849
Compare him to the other characters: Bethany
finally begins to take an interest in the

00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:28.830
world outside herself.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.870
Spencer gets the nerve to take a chance and
kiss Martha, and Martha gets over her shy

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.220
nervousness enough to kiss him back.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.129
Fridge just spent all afternoon playing video
games — but all of his problems are still

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:40.129
there.

00:06:40.129 --> 00:06:42.979
That's kind of how real life works, too.

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:45.020
The Alex paradox

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:49.669
Jumanji is a family movie, so a happy ending
is pretty much par for the course.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:54.370
But let's put on our thinking caps for a second:
the implications of Alex returning to the

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:59.530
moment of his disappearance after being trapped
inside the game for 20 years should probably

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.030
be more severe than the movie lets on.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:04.430
No, seriously, let's talk about this!

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:09.469
If a butterfly flapping its wings in one location
can have a dramatic ripple effect on everything

00:07:09.469 --> 00:07:14.729
else in the world, then a human teenager going
missing would fundamentally change the fabric

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:17.180
of at least Brantford, New Hampshire.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:21.949
Alex's unexplained disappearance from this
small town was a major cultural touchstone

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:23.659
for everyone who lived there.

00:07:23.659 --> 00:07:27.729
It's mentioned multiple times by different
characters at the start of the film, and it's

00:07:27.729 --> 00:07:31.879
the sort of event that would have all kinds
of small effects on people's' relationships

00:07:31.879 --> 00:07:33.150
and behavior.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.590
Parents probably have his disappearance in
the back of their minds while they're raising

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:37.590
their kids.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.840
Old Man Vreeke, who was once vigorous and
full of life, has his destiny dramatically

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.199
changed by the anguish from mysteriously losing
his son.

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:50.169
The disappearance leads other people in town
to be more cautious over the years that follow.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:54.039
The ending should have been more like Back
to the Future, with the tiniest changes in

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.379
the past having major effects in the years
that followed.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:58.120
But nope!

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:00.800
That never happened.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:05.580
Even after the timeline where Spencer, Fridge,
Martha, and Bethany were living in gets winked

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.240
out of existence and replaced by this new
world where Alex never went missing, everything

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.469
outside of Alex's family ends up being exactly
the same.

00:08:14.469 --> 00:08:19.279
Beyond the confines of his household, it appears
that Alex's absence from the world meant just

00:08:19.279 --> 00:08:20.279
about nothing.

00:08:20.279 --> 00:08:21.340
That's insane!

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.340
Right?!

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:29.259
"At no point in your rambling, incoherent
response, were you even close to anything

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:31.460
that could be considered a rational thought."

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.060
Look, okay, fine, sure.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:37.070
We realize this is a movie about a magical
video game, but that still just doesn't make

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.360
a whole lot of sense.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.360
Okay?

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:42.780
Thanks for watching!

00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:45.770
